  "notes": "Test data generation assumptions:\n\n1. **Physical Realism**: All temperatures in Kelvin (>0K), typical range 173.15K (-100\u00b0C) to 373.15K (100\u00b0C). Frozen conditions use temps < 273.15K.\n\n2. **Dimensional Consistency**: Within each test case, array dimensions are consistent:\n   - n_columns = 2 for most tests (allows comparison)\n   - n_levels varies: 3-8 layers depending on test scenario\n   - zi has n_levels+1 elements (layer interfaces)\n\n3. **Depth Arrays**: z (layer centers) < zi boundaries, with zi[0]=0 (surface) and increasing monotonically. dz values sum approximately to depth range.\n\n4. **Moisture Content**: h2osoi_liq + h2osoi_ice represents total water. Frozen soil has more ice, warm soil has more liquid. Values scaled by layer thickness.\n\n5. **Snow Layers**: snl is negative (e.g., -3 = 3 snow layers). When snl < 0, frac_sno > 0 and h2osno > 0.\n\n6. **Thermal Properties**:\n   - Sandy soil: low porosity (0.30-0.35), high tkmg (4-5), high tkdry (0.35-0.48)\n   - Clay soil: high porosity (0.50-0.60), low tkmg (1.8-2.2), low tkdry (0.10-0.15)\n   - csol typical range: 1.2-3.0 MJ/m\u00b3/K\n\n7. **Heat Flux**: Positive = downward (heating soil), negative = upward (cooling soil). Range: -200 to +200 W/m\u00b2.\n\n8. **Time Steps**: 60s (fine resolution) to 3600s (hourly) depending on scenario.\n\n9. **Edge Cases**:\n   - Zero flux: tests numerical stability at equilibrium\n   - Extreme cold: -100\u00b0C, all ice, maximum snow\n   - Dry soil: minimal moisture, high temperature\n   - Thin layers: tests spatial resolution limits\n   - Phase transition: temperatures near 0\u00b0C with mixed phases\n\n10. **Bedrock**: nbedrock index indicates first bedrock layer (different thermal properties below this).\n\n11. **params**: SoilTemperatureParams is a NamedTuple with physical constants (not expanded here but would include denh2o=1000, tfrz=273.15, etc.)."
